## Package comparison

The package remains aligned with approved Phase 3 and this round stays within the server seam. Faulted `close()` now waits for the scoped graph, forwarder-start hooks run before the fibers are forked, and the post-bind test verifies path/provider cleanup before rejection. Most required Phase 3 acceptance work remains outstanding.

## Findings

### High — The required lifecycle and error matrix remains substantially absent

The suite still has only eight facade tests, and the coder result explicitly lists major package gates as residual work. Missing deterministic evidence includes direct Layer/provider/source/listener acquisition counts; complete two-client hello/status/state replay; blocked command and blocked sample shutdown; acceptance racing close; late-write suppression; injected connection/write defects; injected close/unlink failures and `ENOENT`; cleanup continuation and repeated failed-close outcomes; and signal-handler removal. These are explicit Phase 3 acceptance requirements and cannot be deferred.

### High — The socket dependency seam cannot inject required cleanup failures

`layerWithHooks` exposes lifecycle callbacks and the raw listener, but `closeServer` and `unlinkOwnedPath` remain hardwired module functions. Focused tests therefore cannot deterministically inject close failure, non-`ENOENT` unlink failure, or `ENOENT` while still performing real cleanup, as the package requires. Factor these Node operations into the narrow in-file dependency constructor and add the complete cleanup-continuation/idempotency matrix.

### High — Expected close/unlink failures are still converted to defects

The listener finalizer captures `MusicSessionSocketError` values and then uses `Effect.die` (`server.ts:459-460`). This preserves an object for cause squashing but classifies expected socket-boundary failures as defects instead of typed failures. The package requires stable schema-tagged close/unlink errors at the Layer, Promise, and process boundaries. The absent failure-injection tests currently do not establish those semantics or multiple-failure retention.

### Medium — Connection-failure production diagnostics remain lossy

`onConnectionFailure` gives focused tests a synchronous observation point, but production observation still uses a capacity-one sliding queue. Concurrent failures can overwrite earlier causes before the logging fiber observes them. A non-optional bounded counter/metric or equivalent synchronous diagnostic record is needed if every contained defect is to be observed without unbounded payload retention.

### Medium — Test hooks can throw directly from Node callbacks

`onListener`, `onAccepted`, and `onEnrolled` are invoked directly in Node acquisition/connection callbacks. A throwing test hook can escape the scoped Effect boundary, contrary to the server's rule that callback defects be observed and isolated. Wrap hook invocation at the same Effect/runtime boundary or constrain the seam so hooks cannot throw into Node.

